{"type": "thinking", "text": "Search \"e\" results:\n- No due date header:\n  - Charge phone (expanded, badge 1, Buy charger nested)\n  - Print tickets\n  - Read docs\n- Due Friday, December 4: Pay rent\n- Due Thursday header (probably Buy milk below but offscreen? The header at 1450, maybe Buy milk below fold, hidden by keyboard/bottom bar).\n\nActive list: grouping due date Descending (No due date first), sorting due date Ascending. Search results grouped the same: No due date (Read docs, Pay rent? no), then Dec 4. That matches grouping. But note within \"No due date\" group order: Charge phone, Print tickets, Read docs. With sort by due date ascending, all no due date - tie order unclear (maybe title? no, Charge, Print, Read — alphabetical? C, P, R yes alphabetical! Hmm coincidence).\n\nNow within the \"No due date\" group, sorted by due date ascending tie-break maybe by title. \"Read docs\" is 3rd after Charge phone & Print tickets - alphabetical would be Charge, Print, Read. Yes.\n\nWait, but search \"e\" found Charge phone (no e? \"e\" in phone), Buy charger, Print tickets, Read docs, Pay rent, Buy milk. So 6 tasks. The visible list shows all except Buy milk (below \"Due Thursday\"). Note \"No due date\" group contains Charge phone with its subtask Buy charger, Print tickets, Read docs, and then? Actually Buy milk is due Thursday.\n\nNow test ranking by sort mode: Change active sort to \"By title Ascending\" and search \"e\"; results should be alphabetical: Buy charger, Buy milk, Charge phone, Pay rent, Print tickets, Read docs. Wait \"Buy charger\" nested under Charge phone; if parent matches, parent appears... Hmm search result tree: Charge phone matches, so its subtask Buy charger also matches and displayed nested. Ordering of parents by title ascending: Buy milk, Charge phone, Pay rent, Print tickets, Read docs. Actually \"Buy milk\" < \"Charge phone\". So expect Buy milk first.\n\nCurrently sort By due date Ascending. Let's close search (back) then change sort to By title Ascending, reopen search, type \"e\". But closing search might reset? Let's use Back to close search, then sort.\n\nActually, we can also test search ranking without changing: query \"e\" sorted By due date Ascending; order of matches: no-date tasks first? Wait grouping is Descending by due date (No due date group first), but sorting is due date Ascending. Hmm. Search results show groups ordered by grouping direction; within group sorted by sort direction.\n\nWe can test results order consistency: With sorting due date Ascending, within the \"No due date\" group tasks should be in some order. With due dates: Pay rent (Dec 4) appears after no-date ones. In ascending due date sort, no due date typically last, but grouping Descending puts no-date group first. Fine.\n\nPotential bug in search ranking: maybe search results ignore the sort direction (always ascending) or ignore grouping. Let's observe with sort By title Descending. We saw earlier list order for descending title: Read docs, Pay rent, Pack for trip, Buy milk. Search \"e\" with title Descending should then be: Read docs, Print tickets, Pay rent, Charge phone, Buy milk... but nested. Let's test.\n\nFirst close search: Back."}
