{"type": "thinking", "text": "Search \"e\" with sort By title Descending and grouping By due date Descending:\nResults:\nNo due date group: Read docs, Print tickets, Charge phone (with Buy charger nested)\nDec 4 group: Pay rent\nThu group: Buy milk (below keyboard? header at 1450, Buy milk likely hidden behind keyboard/bottom).\n\nThis matches descending title ranking (Read docs > Print tickets > Charge phone; within group). Good — so search is ranked by sort mode as changelog says. But is \"ranked by sort mode\" the desired behavior? The changelog says changed to rank by sort mode instead of relevance. It's implemented. But is there a bug? Let's test direction: We set title Descending, and results descending. So direction respected.\n\nHmm wait, tag grouping: Search results show group headers by due date? There's \"No due date\", \"Due Friday, December 4\", \"Due Thursday\". Yes same as list.\n\nBut here's a potential bug: search was supposed to rank by sort mode, but search results within the \"No due date\" group order descending = Read docs, Print tickets, Charge phone. That's correct.\n\nHmm, maybe the bug is when sorting \"By due date\" but grouping \"None\" etc. Or when sort is \"By priority\" all same.\n\nLet me test search ranking with sort By title Ascending vs Descending to ensure direction. Already did Descending. Let me test sorting By due date Descending (in list), then search \"e\": expect order by due date descending within groups: Dec 4 Pay rent, Thu Buy milk, then No due date (but grouping descending puts no due date first). Within No due date group ties.\n\nActually the key suspicious item: \"matches are now ranked by the sort mode active in the task list rather than by relevance.\" A bug could be that search results are ranked by *sort mode's default direction*, not the active direction. Or by the list's sorting but ignoring grouping. We saw direction respected (descending title gave descending results). Hmm.\n\nWait, maybe the search results ignore the \"sort mode\" for subtasks? Or the search result order is reversed relative to list? Hard.\n\nLet me test with a query where relevance ordering differs clearly. Query \"e\" matches all tasks; results sorted by title desc. Good.\n\nWhat about search \"buy\": matches Buy milk and Buy charger (nested). Ordering irrelevant.\n\nMaybe test sorting \"By due date Ascending\" with search \"e\": expected within groups ascending dates: Buy milk (Thu) first, Pack for trip? Pack for trip does it match \"e\"? \"Pack for trip\" no e. Hmm. Query \"r\": matches \"Pack for trip\" (r), \"Pay rent\" (r), \"Read docs\" (r), \"Buy charger\" (r), \"Print tickets\" (r), \"Charge phone\" (r? charge has r), Buy milk? no r? \"2 litres\" notes has r? maybe. Let's use \"r\" and sort due date ascending, grouping due date descending. Expected group order No due date first (grouping desc), within no-date sorted due date asc (tie → maybe title). Then Dec 4, Nov 24, Thu. Hmm.\n\nAlternatively sort By priority all none, ties... useless.\n\nMaybe the seeded bug is in \"direction preference loaded with a new fallback value\". Let's focus. Potential bug: when you choose a sort mode that hasn't been used before, the direction shown is the fallback, but it's wrong (e.g., always Descending for due date). We observed selecting By title gave Ascending; selecting By due date gave Ascending. Hmm, default for title usually ascending, due date ascending maybe. What about \"By priority\" or \"Created\" — default descending maybe? After selecting, direction got Ascending. Could be fine.\n\nAnother: grouping direction persisted as Descending across sessions, and when we set Sorting By title, grouping stayed Descending; sorting direction reset to Ascending. That seems expected: each sort mode has its own remembered direction? Or direction is global for sorting? When we set sorting to By title from By due date Ascending, it showed Ascending; then we toggled Descending; then later changed to By due date -> Ascending (not Descending). So direction is per sort mode or resets, not global. Hmm.\n\nBut maybe bug: the direction preference is saved for one mode and wrongly applied to another. E.g., after setting By title Descending, switching to By due date shows Ascending (reset), good. Then switching back to By title, does it remember Descending? Let's test: reopen, change sorting to By title, see direction. If it shows Ascending rather than remembered Descending, maybe that's the \"new fallback\" bug (direction pref not persisted per mode). But is that a bug? Maybe not.\n\nLet's test: currently sorting By title Descending. Close search, open sort, switch to By due date, then back to By title, see direction. Let's do.\n\nActually wait. There's a much more suspicious thing: the sheet's \"Sorting By title Descending\" but when I earlier selected \"By title\" it defaulted to Ascending, then I chose Descending. Then I opened the sheet later? Not since. Let's check if it persists after rotation and leaving. It showed Descending before. Let's now check after search etc.\n\nBut let me test a possible fallback bug: The direction preference \"loaded with a new fallback value\" might cause the sheet to display \"Ascending\" while the list uses \"Descending\" (or vice versa) on first open after app restart. E.g., persisted direction Descending, but sheet shows Ascending until toggled. Hmm.\n\nWe can force-stop the app? The tools don't directly provide force-stop. We can go to home and open_app; that just foregrounds, process persists. But rotating may recreate activity. We saw values persisted through rotation. App restart could be simulated by going to Settings > Apps? Too much. Could use recent apps swipe away? APP_SWITCH then swipe? That may kill process. Not necessary.\n\nLet's look at other changed area: scheduled-date grouping. Maybe the bug is clearly visible with \"By start date\" grouping when tasks have start dates. All tasks have no start date; we need to set start dates. The due date tasks might have start dates in precondition? No, only due dates. So to test scheduled date grouping we must create tasks with start dates.\n\nLet me create a temporary task with a start date, e.g., \"ZZ Test today\", start date today. And another \"ZZ Test tomorrow\". Then group by start date and see if groups appear (\"Today\", \"Tomorrow\"). This tests comparison rules.\n\nBut what exactly does \"date groups decided by fewer comparison rules\" mean? Maybe the bug is that grouping by start date puts tasks with *different* start dates in same group when they share day-of-month or something. Let's test multiple dates that differ in year/month.\n\nActually, in Tasks.org, \"By start date\" grouping might use \"date\" group headers. Let me test with a task with a start date.\n\nCreating a task: FAB → title, set start date. Then save. Later delete.\n\nAlternatively, we can edit an existing task's start date (e.g., Buy milk) then revert. That modifies existing data; creating temp tasks is cleaner.\n\nLet me plan temp tasks: \"QA Alpha\" start date today, \"QA Beta\" start date tomorrow, \"QA Gamma\" start date same month next day? To test boundary rules, maybe set start date exactly 7/8 days ahead, 14 days etc. But creating many tasks is time-consuming.\n\nMaybe there's an easier way: The \"By start date\" grouping might currently show \"No start date\" group with all tasks. If tasks have start dates, groups might be like \"Today\", \"Tomorrow\", \"Later\", \"No start date\". The change \"date groups are now decided by fewer comparison rules\" likely means date group boundaries got broken (e.g., \"Tomorrow\" included in \"Today\"). A task with start date tomorrow would reveal.\n\nLet me create one temp task with start date tomorrow, and maybe one with today.\n\nFirst, exit search: tap Collapse. Then FAB.\n\nActually, let me first finish search tests. Search itself: query \"guide\" should match Read docs notes. Search \"https\" maybe. Let's test notes search: type \"guide\" clear."}
