{"type": "thinking", "text": "Case-insensitive works.\n\nLet's test whitespace, partial word. \"Buy\" → Buy milk and Buy charger. Fine.\n\nNow let me think about the seed bug more concretely. Perhaps I should look for behavioral mismatches:\n\nHypothesis A (sort direction fallback): The sort direction preference is loaded with a new fallback value that is wrong. Evidence could be: when switching to a new sort mode, direction always \"Ascending\" even for modes whose natural default is Descending (e.g., \"By last modified\" usually descending, \"Created\" usually descending). The fallback \"Ascending\" could be a bug causing newly selected sort modes to sort ascending when they should default descending. But is that observable as defect? Hard to claim.\n\nAlternatively: The grouping direction is \"Descending\" persisted from earlier but when reopening the app it might reset. Let's test app process restart. We can use `open_app` after pressing HOME? It won't reset data but may reload preferences. Let's try pressing HOME, then open_app, then check sort sheet values (title Descending). Preferences should persist regardless.\n\nMaybe the bug appears only in landscape: when rotated, the sheet drops some rows (Move completed tasks to bottom disappeared in landscape). That's a layout issue but maybe not the targeted bug. Actually in landscape the sheet was scrollable? Elements showed only 13 with sub-rows; \"Move completed tasks to bottom\" and \"Completed\" row missing maybe due to scroll cutoff. Not necessarily a bug.\n\nLet's inspect the sheet with screenshot in landscape later perhaps.\n\nHypothesis B (scheduled-date grouping): \"date groups are now decided by fewer comparison rules\" → likely a regression where tasks with different scheduled dates are grouped by comparing only some fields. Since no tasks have start dates, all fall under \"No start date\", so to detect we need start dates. Let's create temp tasks.\n\nPlan for temp tasks with start dates:\n- \"QA A\" with start date today.\n- \"QA B\" with start date tomorrow.\n- \"QA C\" with start date in 8 days.\n- \"QA D\" with start date in 40 days (next month).\nThen group by start date and observe headers. Expected headers likely \"Today\", \"Tomorrow\", and actual dates. If \"fewer comparison rules\" bug uses only day-of-month, then e.g. today=Nov14, tomorrow=Nov15, 8 days=Nov22, 40 days=Dec24. All day-of-month distinct, so no collision. To test comparison rule bug, choose dates with same day-of-month different month/year! But setting start date is through date picker; we can set e.g. Dec 14 (same day as today Nov 14). That would show whether group header uses day only. Hmm but if grouping is per-date header, the headers are formatted dates; same day-of-month different month should be separate groups. If fewer comparison rules compare only day-of-month, they'd merge. That's a plausible bug.\n\nAlternatively, \"fewer comparison rules\" may refer to relative grouping: \"Today/Tomorrow/This week/Next week/Later\" needs comparing year/month/day. Fewer rules could break year boundaries. E.g., Dec 31 vs Jan 1.\n\nBut what does Tasks.org grouping actually look like for start date? Let me set a start date on a temp task and see. Let's create one task first and inspect.\n\nExit search (collapse) then create task via FAB. Steps:\n- Tap Collapse.\n- Tap + FAB.\n- Enter title.\n- Tap \"No start date\" row → date picker.\n- Choose today.\n- Save.\n\nLet's do it."}
