w / Next Sat\". Hmm, that's inconsistent; maybe the first sheet is some other mode like \"start date\" where quick rows are \"Due date\" (copy due date?), \"Due time\" (copy due time?), \"Day before due\", \"Week before due\" — these could literally be start date shortcut options: \"Day before due\" means set start date one day before due date, \"Week before due\" one week before due date. That makes sense! For a start date picker, useful shortcuts include \"Due date\" (same as due date), \"Day before due\", \"Week before due\". And \"Due time\" sets the start time equal to due time. Yes! That's plausible: start date picker shortcuts relative to the due date. So the first sheet is the start date picker with options \"Due date\", \"Due time\", \"Day before due\", \"Week before due\", \"No date\". Hmm, but the label of the sheet itself was \"Due date\" at top... The icon was a calendar and text \"Due date\". That might be the first quick option (copy due date), not a title. Indeed, the second sheet's first quick option is \"Today\" at same position 403. So the sheet has no title; the first row is a quick option. For start date, the first quick option is \"Due date\" (i.e., set start date to the due date). OK! So no bug. Good.\n\nSo in start-date picker, \"Due date\" option at top sets start date equal to due date. Our temp task has no due date, so selecting it might set none. Better to use calendar and choose today (Nov 14).\n\nLet me cancel this due date sheet and go back to start date picker. Tap Cancel (53)."}
