{"type": "thinking", "text": "Confirmed: after saving QA plain (no start date), QQ child is now top-level (x=540, checkbox x=73), Pack badge 2, Charge phone has no badge. So any save of a new task detaches the most recent subtask. This is a genuine bug apparently. But is it actually because of the new task save, or because of \"stale editor state\" from adding subtask? Let's see: Adding QQ child (save on Charge phone) then creating QA plain (save) → detach. The pattern repeats.\n\nHmm, but wait. Maybe there's a different explanation: When I add a subtask via \"Add subtask\" and save the parent, the subtask gets nested. Then when I open a new task editor and save, the app may be saving the *parent Charge phone* again (wrong task) — and since the editor's state didn't include the subtask, it removes it. But the new task also got created. So save affects both? That's weird.\n\nActually, maybe the real cause is something else: the \"Add subtask\" flow when saving leaves the \"parent task\" editor in a state where the subtask is persisted as a draft. Then, the next save commits it as a top-level task (with parent id null). Hmm.\n\nAlternatively, maybe this is expected behavior: The \"Add subtask\" entry might create a *new top-level task* only after the parent is saved, and the nesting we observed was ephemeral? No, data persisted through many screens.\n\nLet me verify the detachment once more in a cleaner way and capture evidence. Actually, we've reproduced twice (Buy charger and ZZ sub, QQ child). This is likely a real bug: creating a new task after adding a subtask causes the subtask to be promoted to top level / lost from parent. This fits the area \"subtasks\" and possibly \"subtask badges\" (badge count drops).\n\nBut before reporting, I want to ensure it's not caused by something I did (e.g., the grouping None + sort title). Let's test once more with default grouping to be sure: Set grouping back to By due date, add a subtask \"RR child\" under Charge phone, save, then create new task \"QA plain2\", save, check. If reproducible, report.\n\nActually, wait. Another thought: maybe the detach happens because of the *sort mode*. With grouping None and sorting By title Descending, subtasks might be sorted independently. When a new task is added and list re-sorts, the subtask is displayed flat because the tree is rebuilt incorrectly due to sorting by title (subtasks lose their parent association in display). But the Charge phone badge disappearing is stronger evidence of data change... unless the badge is computed from the currently loaded flat list, and when sorting by title, subtasks are not recognized as children. Hmm! The changelog says badge number computed from full list of nested tasks. Maybe with grouping None, the badge computation is different.\n\nWait, when we saved QQ child, with the same grouping None and title sorting, the badge was 3 and QQ child nested. Then saving QA plain changed it. Hmm, so it's not sort mode alone.\n\nLet me test: Without adding any subtask, just save a new task, see if existing ZZ sub / QQ child (top-level now) changes. Not useful.\n\nLet me test the hypothesis \"saving a new task re-saves the last edited parent and drops the newly added subtask\" by observing whether the subtask is actually still in DB but shows top-level. We can check Charge phone's edit screen after detach: earlier it showed no subtasks. So parent-child link lost.\n\nCould this be due to a \"save\" race where the new subtask is created with a temporary ID and then, when creating a new task, the ID gets reassigned? Hmm.\n\nAlternatively, maybe the app is behaving as designed: in Tasks.org, when you add a subtask to a task and then tap Save (top-left), the subtask is created. But maybe the *default* behavior when you tap the FAB from the list is to add a task, and the \"Add subtask\" draft persists; the next save flushes it. Hmm.\n\nRegardless, from a user's perspective: After adding a subtask and it appears nested, adding a new task later makes the subtask appear as a top-level task. That's a data bug. But the QA areas: subtasks and subtask badges. Good.\n\nHowever, I'm a bit worried that this is due to my specific interaction with the start-date picker on the new task, or due to the app's task editor auto-saving. Let me do a rigorous minimal repro with screenshots and no other actions:\n\nState: Charge phone has no subtasks (QQ child detached). Pack badge 2.\n1. Expand Pack (already expanded).\n2. Tap Charge phone → Add subtask → type \"RR child\" → Save.\n3. Observe: Pack badge 3, Charge badge 1, RR child nested.\n4. Tap FAB → type \"QA plain2\" → Save (no date).\n5. Observe: Pack badge ? and RR child position.\n\nIf it reproduces, that's solid. Let's do it. Actually, maybe I can also test the reverse: save an *existing* task after adding subtask (save again) — does it detach? Let's test later.\n\nStep 2: tap Charge phone row (element 21)."}
