{"type": "thinking", "text": "Reproduced again with no start date. After saving QA plain2, RR child is top-level, Pack badge 2, Charge phone no badge. So creating a new task detaches the most recently added subtask, regardless of date.\n\nWait... OR maybe the detachment happens when the list refreshes after *any* save, and the \"most recently added subtask\" is the one I just added. But QQ child and ZZ sub also detached earlier on save. So the pattern is consistent: after adding a subtask to Charge phone and saving, then adding a new task and saving, the subtask pops out to top-level.\n\nActually, maybe this is even simpler: The subtask is only a *draft* until the next save? No, it persisted through multiple steps (search etc.).\n\nHmm, hold on. Let me reconsider something: In the list after saving RR child, RR child was displayed nested under Charge phone. But maybe that nested display was only because the list was in \"My order\" and the subtask was added to the *editor's in-memory task*, and the list's local cache rendered it. Then saving a new task triggers a full reload from DB, where the subtask was actually stored without parent. I.e., the \"Add subtask\" via editor Save may not persist parent link properly, and the UI shows nested from memory until reload. The new task save triggers reload, revealing the true state: orphan top-level.\n\nBut the first time, Buy charger was nested after save and persisted through many screens (search, sort, etc.). Those might not reload from DB. Then the new task save reloaded. Hmm, but the app's list should reload on every save. Yet after saving Charge phone with the subtask, it showed nested, so the DB likely had the link at that point (the app reloaded and displayed nested). Unless the app didn't reload.\n\nAnyway, from a user perspective, the subtask's parent assignment doesn't persist across a reload. The badge count (which is supposed to be computed from the full nested list) similarly drops from 3 to 2. This is a real bug.\n\nBut wait: could it be that the \"detachment\" is actually caused by the new task save re-saving the subtask's *parent* as a new task? No, RR child became top-level, not the parent.\n\nLet me check Charge phone's detail now: does it list any subtask? Earlier after detachment it showed none. Let's confirm for RR child: open Charge phone and see (maybe subtask not listed). We'll do that later.\n\nActually, perhaps I should check whether the detached subtask is still a child in the DB but displayed flat due to sorting? The Charge phone detail would show subtasks; earlier it didn't list Buy charger (so DB link lost). Let's verify with RR child.\n\nBefore that, since this bug is significant, let me file it maybe after confirming the details. But first, I want to be sure this isn't an artifact of my clicking \"Save\" in a strange way. The app's expected flow for adding subtasks may be: tap \"Add subtask\" → type → press Enter or tap the checkmark → the subtask is immediately saved to DB. Tapping the top \"Save\" may save the parent and close, which we did. Hmm.\n\nActually, maybe I misused the UI: maybe when you tap \"Add subtask\", a new subtask row appears with an empty field; typing there and pressing \"Save\" creates the subtask, but the proper way is to press \"Done\" (keyboard check) to add it, then Save. Pressing Save may create it in a weird state. But the UI showed it nested, so it seemed fine.\n\nHmm, the fact that the detachment happens on the *next* save suggests the subtask was saved as a \"new task\" with a parent field that is only set in memory. This might be an actual bug in this build.\n\nHowever, wait: Could it be that the *new task* I created with the FAB is inheriting the parent from the last \"Add subtask\" draft? And the \"detached\" subtask is actually a *duplicate* created by the new-task save? Let's check: After saving RR child and then QA plain2, list shows ZZ sub, RR child, QQ child top-level. Are there duplicates? No duplicate RR child. So not duplicate.\n\nAlternatively, the new task save may have \"moved\" RR child to top-level because the new task editor wrote parent=null to the last-created task (RR child) instead of the new task? But QA plain2 was created correctly (top-level). Hmm.\n\nLet me look for another explanation: Maybe the \"saved\" subtask was never attached to Charge phone but was attached to *Pack for trip* directly (as a sibling)? The badge count 3 on Pack for trip and Charge phone badge 1 showed it under Charge phone. But maybe the badge counts descendants, and RR child was a direct child of Pack for trip, not Charge phone. The indentation x=592 vs Charge phone x=566 suggests 3rd level (child of Charge phone). So it was Charge phone's child.\n\nOK, likely a genuine defect. But the changelog said \"Subtask badges on task rows: the badge number is now computed from the full list of nested tasks.\" The bug could be exactly this: the badge is computed from a stale/incomplete nested list, dropping orphaned tasks. Hmm, but the data detachment is more severe.\n\nHold on! Maybe the detachment is a *display artifact* of \"Move completed tasks to bottom\" or \"Subtasks: My order\"? No, badge dropped too.\n\nLet me test another important subtask behavior: complete a subtask and see badge. Earlier completing Buy charger left badge 3 (expanded with completed shown). Hmm.\n\nLet me refocus: The seeded bugs are likely in the four changed features. Maybe this detachment thing is not seeded; it might be a real app quirk caused by my flow. But the QA task says find bugs. A reproducible data bug is worth reporting even if not seeded. But I should be careful about false positives. The developer will reply; if rejected, fine.\n\nActually, let me reconsider the detachment via a different route: perhaps the \"Add subtask\" within the task editor is not fully implemented in this build, and the subtask only gets saved when the *next* save occurs. Hmm.\n\nWait, here's a simpler explanation that fits perfectly: When you tap \"Add subtask\" and type a title, then tap the top-left \"Save\" button, maybe the parent task is saved *without* the new subtask (the subtask input is ignored). But the list UI shows the subtask nested because the editor screen's result returns the in-memory task with subtask, and the list updates from that returned object. Then when the list is reloaded from the DB (triggered by creating another task), the subtask is gone from the parent... but where does it come from as a top-level task? Ah, maybe the subtask was saved as a *separate new task* at the moment of Save (top-level), and the in-memory parent also got it as child for display, but DB only has it top-level. Upon reload, it appears top-level. That fits! So the bug: adding a subtask via the editor and tapping Save persists it as a top-level task, while the UI temporarily shows it nested. On next reload, it's top-level and parent badge decreases.\n\nOK. But again, maybe pressing Enter should be used to commit the subtask, and then Save. Let me test the \"correct\" flow: Add subtask, type title, press the keyboard Done/Enter, then maybe the subtask is committed and a new empty row appears; then tap Save. Earlier pressing ENTER inserted a newline, which suggests Done wasn't wired to create the subtask either. Hmm, in the app, the subtask row likely has a checkmark on the left (element 52 Button at 222,1467) — maybe tapping that button creates the subtask. The row had a Button (checkbox?) and EditText and an X (delete). The Button at left might be a \"done/check\" button to save the subtask. Let me test this proper flow! Maybe tapping that left button (element) creates and saves the subtask immediately, and then Save. If that works, then my earlier flow was not the intended one, but still a UI bug (Save should save the subtask too).\n\nLet's test: open Charge phone, Add subtask, type \"SS child\", tap the left button (the square/checkbox at 222,1467?) to commit, then Save. Then create a new task and see if it stays nested. If it stays nested, then the \"correct\" flow works and the bug is that tapping Save doesn't persist the subtask. Still a bug but maybe less severe. Actually, if tapping Save doesn't persist subtask, that's a real bug: user adds subtask, taps Save, sees it nested, but later i", "truncated": true}
