{"type": "thinking", "text": "Reproduced again: SS child now top-level; Pack badge 2. So solidly reproducible: after adding a subtask and saving, creating a new task (any) causes the subtask to become top-level and the parent badge to decrease. This is a serious subtask persistence bug. We should report.\n\nBut wait, maybe the true cause is that *tapping Save on the parent editor doesn't persist the new subtask as a child*, and the list is fine until a reload. The new task save triggers a reload. Either way, the observed symptom: subtask added under Charge phone later appears as top-level task; Pack for trip badge decreases from 3 to 2.\n\nLet me now verify by *just reopening the list* or switching away and back (without creating a new task) whether the subtask stays nested. This will clarify: does a simple refresh (rotation or app reopen) also detach? If yes, the bug is definitely \"subtask not persisted\". If only new task save detaches, weird but still bug.\n\nTest: add subtask \"TT child\" to Charge phone, save, then rotate device (activity recreate) and see if it detaches. Rotation earlier didn't affect list data? We can test.\n\nAlternatively, exit app and reopen (open_app) — process may persist. Let's use rotation as a reload. First, current state: Charge phone no subtasks. Add TT child, save, note badge 3, then rotate to landscape and back, check if TT child remains nested. Let's do it.\n\nCurrent list is expanded Pack. Tap Charge phone (29)."}
