{"type": "thinking", "text": "Rotation didn't detach TT child; still nested (Pack badge 3, Charge badge 1). So rotation/reload doesn't break it, only saving a new task does. Interesting.\n\nHmm, wait. Maybe the cause is not \"new task save\" but \"saving QA plainN while Charge phone's editor has unsaved state\"? No, the Charge phone editor was closed.\n\nLet me think about what's special about creating a new task after adding a subtask. Perhaps the app's \"new task\" editor reuses the *same task ID* as the just-created subtask? Like, the new task gets the subtask's ID? No.\n\nAnother possibility: The app's list has a \"My order\" sorting and when creating a new task it recalculates sort order. Maybe subtasks whose parents are \"not in the list\" get promoted? No, Charge phone is in the list.\n\nHmm, maybe it's the \"Subtasks: My order\" + \"Grouping: None\" combo. When grouping is None, subtasks may be shown flat in the list when re-sorted by title. But then the badge count... Let's check: with grouping None, the badge on Pack for trip might be computed by subtracting visible top-level tasks? Hmm.\n\nWait, current grouping None and sort By title Descending. The list shows top-level tasks (ZZ sub, SS child, RR child, Read docs, QQ child, ...) and nested tasks (Charge phone, TT child, Print tickets). TT child remains nested. So grouping None doesn't imply flat subtasks.\n\nLet me test whether *creating a new task* detaches the subtask when grouping is None vs By due date. Maybe the bug only occurs when grouping None + sort By title. But we should test with grouping By due date. That's more typical. But previous detachments all occurred with grouping None. Hmm! Important: All our detachment observations happened while grouping = None and sorting = By title Descending. We never tested creating a new task with grouping By due date after adding a subtask. So the \"bug\" may be specific to grouping None? Or maybe the new task save isn't the cause; perhaps changing sort/grouping after adding a subtask causes the subtask to be reparented? But we did change sort (title) *before* adding subtasks, not after. Hmm.\n\nWait—when we add a subtask, the list re-sorts by title. TT child is nested. Then creating a task re-sorts. TT child stays? No, it detached.\n\nLet me test creating a new task with grouping None but sorting By due date (or By title Ascending) to see. Alternatively switch grouping to By due date, then add subtask UU, then create new task, check. This will tell whether grouping None is the trigger.\n\nActually, maybe the \"detachment\" is caused by sorting subtasks \"My order\" not being available when grouping is None? Hmm.\n\nLet's be systematic. Current state grouping None, sort title Desc. TT child nested under Charge phone.\n\nTest 1: Create new task \"QA plain4\" and save → expect TT child detaches (per pattern). Confirm.\nTest 2: Set grouping By due date, add \"UU child\" under Charge phone, save, create new task \"QA plain5\", see if detaches.\n\nBut this is a lot of steps. Alternatively, maybe first check the sort sheet: does changing grouping to By due date after adding a subtask detach it? If changing settings detaches, that's a different bug (settings change causes data loss). Let me test with TT child: change grouping to By due date and see if TT child stays nested. If it detaches, then the \"save new task\" is a red herring and the detach happens on any list reconfiguration.\n\nHmm, but we changed sort/grouping multiple times earlier while Buy charger existed, and it stayed nested (search results etc.). So group/sort changes alone don't detach. Let me verify with TT child anyway: open sort, change grouping to By due date, close, check TT child.\n\nLet's do it. Open sort (element 35)."}
