{"type": "thinking", "text": "Now we see \"Start today\" with QA top, QA today; \"Start tomorrow\" with QA tomorrow; \"Start Tuesday, November 24\" with QA next; \"No start date\" with rest. Groups look correct and separated.\n\nHmm, but \"Start Tuesday, November 24\" — interesting format: it includes weekday only for dates within a week? Tomorrow shows \"Start tomorrow\"; today \"Start today\". The format is fine.\n\nNow the change \"Scheduled-date grouping option: the date groups are now decided by fewer comparison rules.\" Maybe the bug relates to grouping dates with same day-of-month or overlapping relative labels. Our dates: today, tomorrow, Nov 24. All distinct. Groups correct.\n\nLet's test boundary: What about a start date 7 days from today (Nov 21, next Saturday)? The header might be \"Start next Saturday\" or \"Start Saturday, November 21\". And a start date Nov 22 (8 days). We need to see if any two distinct dates get merged into one group due to fewer comparison rules. The \"fewer comparison rules\" could mean comparing only the day (e.g., \"Saturday\" vs...). Hmm.\n\nActually, consider this likely bug: With \"By start date\" grouping, the group is decided by comparing only the date's *day-of-month* or *weekday* rather than full date. Tasks with start date today (Nov 14) and start date Dec 14 would be grouped together. To test, create a task with start date Dec 14 (one month from today) and see whether it merges with \"Start today\". That's a good test of \"fewer comparison rules\".\n\nSimilarly, a task start date Nov 14 next year (Nov 14, 2027) vs today. But the \"fewer comparison rules\" specifically says \"date groups are now decided by fewer comparison rules\". Could be comparing (year, month, day) vs just day. Let's test Dec 14 2026.\n\nCreate \"QA month\" with start date Dec 14, 2026. Steps: FAB → title → start date row → next month arrow → select Dec 14 → OK → Save. That's ~6 steps. Worth it.\n\nBut before that, note the current grouping is By start date Ascending, and sorting By title Descending. Temp tasks are numerous. Let's create \"QA month\" (Dec 14).\n\nFAB (30)."}
