{"type": "thinking", "text": "QA year (Nov 14, 2027) got its own group \"Start Sunday, November 14, 2027\" — separate from \"Start today\". So grouping by start date correctly compares year/month/day. No bug there. Good.\n\nSo scheduled-date grouping seems fine. Maybe the bug is elsewhere: the \"fewer comparison rules\" might relate to *due date* grouping relative labels (\"Today\", \"Tomorrow\", \"Next Sat\")? Hmm. Or maybe grouping by start date with tasks that are *completed*, or with start dates at specific times. Hmm.\n\nWait, maybe the \"scheduled-date grouping option\" refers to \"By start date\" but the grouping groups tasks by *day only*, ignoring time. Our tasks all have start time default \"No time\". What if a task has a start date with a time? Then tasks on the same date but different times should group together (day grouping). Hmm.\n\nAlternatively, \"date groups are now decided by fewer comparison rules\" might mean the grouping *header* format omits some fields (e.g., year) but still separates groups. Our Nov 14 2027 group included year, so no.\n\nMaybe the bug is in the due-date grouping: \"Due Thursday\" label omits the date/month, while Nov 24 includes \"November 24\". Why would \"Due Thursday\" not include \"November 19\"? In Tasks.org, group headers format dates relative to today: \"Due Thursday\" for within a week (next Thursday), \"Due Tuesday, November 24\" for further. This is normal.\n\nHmm. Could the bug be that the *same date* tasks with different times create separate groups? Not in our data.\n\nLet's revisit the sort sheet direction fallback. That's a changed area we haven't found a bug in yet. Maybe there is one. The \"direction preference is now loaded with a new fallback value\" could mean: when the sort mode has no saved direction, use a default; if the default is wrong for some modes (e.g., By priority defaults Ascending but should Descending), that's a bug. But how to know expected? Common defaults: By due date ascending, By start date ascending, By priority descending (highest first), By title ascending, By last modified descending, Created descending, Smart sort? Hmm. When we selected each mode, the direction showed \"Ascending\" for By due date and By title. We didn't check By priority or last modified or Created.\n\nLet's test selecting \"By priority\" and see direction. If it shows Ascending, that might be the bug (priority should be descending: high priority first). But can we verify behavior? We can set task priorities to test ordering. Hmm.\n\nMaybe easier: The sort direction fallback might be about *grouping* direction. When grouping is None, there is no direction row. When grouping is By due date, the direction row appears. We saw grouping direction persisted as Descending and Ascending. Seems fine.\n\nWait, maybe the bug: For the \"Completed\" sorting row, direction preference loaded with fallback \"Descending\" always, even after user changes it. Let's test: change \"Completed\" direction to Ascending, close, reopen, see if it persists. But completed tasks none. Hard to verify order.\n\nHmm. Let's think about \"direction preference is now loaded with a new fallback value.\" In Tasks.org, the sort dialog's direction toggle for sorting may have a preference stored per sort mode. The fallback loads e.g. `SortDirection.ASC` instead of `DESC` or vice versa. A visible bug would be: on opening the sort sheet, the direction shows one value, but the list is sorted in the opposite direction. We saw \"Sorting By title Descending\" with list order descending (Read docs...), consistent. And \"By due date Ascending\" gave ascending. So no mismatch.\n\nMaybe after restarting the app, preference loaded wrong. Let's test app restart: press HOME, then open_app (process may persist), or better use APP_SWITCH to kill. Actually, `open_app` just foregrounds without reset; if process killed by system, it reloads. We can try pressing HOME then open_app, maybe enough. But to truly reload, could clear from recents. Let's try: press HOME, then APP_SWITCH, swipe the app card away, then open_app. That might kill the process and reload preferences. Let's attempt later.\n\nBefore that, let's examine sort behavior with \"By priority\" and \"By last modified\" to see if any direction inconsistency. But need priorities on tasks. We can set priorities to temp tasks. Hmm.\n\nMaybe simpler: look at the sort sheet after selecting \"By priority\" — the direction might show \"Ascending\", which is likely wrong because priority sorting in Tasks.org usually descending (highest priority first). Let me check: In Tasks.org, default sort for priority is descending? The sort options likely \"Priority\" with \"Ascending\"/\"Descending\". In many task apps, sorting by priority ascending means lowest priority first, which is unintuitive; default should be descending. The changelog says direction preference loaded with a new fallback value; if fallback is Ascending for all modes, then priority sort may be broken (lowest first), but that's not clearly a bug without expectations.\n\nLet's test by giving two tasks different priorities and see sort behavior. We have many temp tasks. Set \"QA top\" priority High (red) and \"QA today\" priority Low (blue). Then sort By priority Ascending/Descending and see order. If direction toggle works, fine. If direction display doesn't match order, bug.\n\nBut that's a lot of steps. Maybe the seeded bug for direction is simpler: When you choose a sort mode and then close/reopen, the direction resets to fallback instead of persisting. We observed \"By title Descending\" persisted across reopening sheets (after several opens). So not.\n\nBut maybe it resets after app restart. Let's test app restart now: currently grouping By start date, sort By title Descending. Let's record. Press HOME, then open_app. Check sheet values. If they persist, fine.\n\nLet's do HOME then open_app."}
