{"type": "thinking", "text": "Smart sort Descending. Hmm.\n\nMaybe direction fallback bug is not in these defaults. Let's set sorting back to By due date Ascending for consistency and continue with search ranking tests / subtask badge tests.\n\nActually, wait: There might be a bug where the direction is not applied to the list in some cases. Let's verify sorting by \"By last modified\" or \"Created\" direction actually reorders. But all tasks have similar modified times; hard.\n\nAlternative approach: maybe the \"direction preference\" bug is that when you change the *sort mode*, the direction is loaded from the new mode's saved preference, but the list doesn't reload. We saw it reload correctly (title/due date). Hmm.\n\nMaybe the bug is when direction is changed while the sheet is open, the *grouping* direction changes too? No.\n\nLet's move on. Search ranking test with multiple sort modes was done for title. Maybe there's a bug in search when sorting by due date: the search results might be ordered by relevance still, or not by due date. We tested \"e\" with sort By due date Ascending and grouping By due date Descending; results within each group might have been arbitrary because ties. Need a query that matches tasks with different due dates and sort By due date Descending to see clear order. Let's craft: query \"r\" matches:\n- Read docs (no due)\n- Pay rent (Dec 4)\n- Pack for trip (Nov 24) — \"r\" in trip\n- Charge phone (no due)\n- Print tickets (no due? \"tickets\" no r; \"Print\" has r) yes\n- Buy charger (no due)\n- Buy milk? no r (notes \"2 litres\" has r? yes \"litres\" contains r)\nSo many. Sort By due date Descending: within group? Grouping by due date Descending currently? We set grouping By start date. Let's set grouping None to isolate sort. Then search \"r\" with sort By due date Descending → expected order by due date desc: Pay rent (Dec 4), Pack for trip (Nov 24), Buy milk (Nov 19), then no-due tasks. With grouping None and descending, tasks without due date maybe first or last? Earlier with due date Descending grouping (null first?) Hmm. When sorting By due date Descending with grouping None, no-due tasks placement? Let's test and compare between list and search.\n\nActually, the search ranking change: \"matches are now ranked by the sort mode active in the task list rather than by relevance.\" If sort is By due date Descending, search results should reflect that. We can verify by comparing the order of matching tasks in search vs their order in the unfiltered list. If they match, implementation works.\n\nLet's do a robust test:\n1. Set grouping None, sorting By due date Descending.\n2. Close sheet, note full list order.\n3. Open search, query \"r\", note order of same tasks.\n4. Compare.\n\nWe need to handle subtask nesting. With grouping None, \"Pack for trip\" is a parent with children; search results may show parents and nested children. Let's use a query that matches only top-level tasks: \"a\" matches Pay rent? \"a\" in Pay, Pack, Read docs, Buy milk? no a? \"milk\" no, \"2 litres\" no a. QA tasks (temp) too. Hmm, temp tasks pollute. Could use query \"p\" matches Pack for trip, Pay rent, Print tickets, Charge phone? (phone has p), QA plain... temp.\n\nMaybe simpler: ignore temp tasks but they'll appear. We can still compare relative order.\n\nAlternatively, delete temp tasks now to clean up and make search tests cleaner. There are many temp tasks (ZZ sub, TT child, SS child, RR child, QQ child, QA top, QA today, QA tomorrow, QA next, QA month, QA year, QA plain..QA plain4, Buy charger). That's 16 tasks! Deleting each is tedious (open, Delete task, confirm). Maybe too many steps but worthwhile? The instructions say don't change data outside testing; we've added a lot. We should clean up, but it may take many steps. We have enough steps? Possibly. But first finish testing.\n\nFor search ranking, temp tasks may help because they have varied start dates but not due dates. They may obscure. We can use a query matching only original tasks, e.g., \"k\" matches \"Pack for trip\", \"Buy milk\"? \"milk\" has k. \"Read docs\" no. \"Pay rent\" no. Charge phone no. Print tickets has k? \"tickets\" has k. Buy charger has k? \"charger\" no k? c,h,a,r,g,e,r no k. \"QA\" tasks no k. So \"k\" matches Pack for trip, Buy milk, Print tickets. Sort by due date Descending: Pack for trip (Nov 24), Buy milk (Thu Nov 19), Print tickets (no due date). With grouping None and sorting due date Descending, no-due tasks placement? Let's observe list order for these three: Pack for trip (parent), its child Print tickets (nested), Buy milk. If no-due tasks come first in descending (as seen earlier with grouping Descending), then Print tickets might be first? But nested under Pack. Hmm.\n\nBetter: query \"k\" matches \"Buy milk\" (k), \"Pack for trip\" (k), \"Print tickets\" (k). Search results with sort By due date Descending and grouping None: If no-due tie placement puts Print tickets first? But Print is a child of Pack which also matches, so shown nested.\n\nHmm. Let's instead set sorting By due date Ascending: original order clear: Buy milk (Thu), Pack for trip (Nov 24) with Print tickets nested, Pay rent, Read docs. Query \"k\": Buy milk, Pack for trip (+ Print tickets). Expected search order: Buy milk then Pack for trip. With title Ranking, \"k\" results relevance unknown. This could show if sort applied. But both orders (ascending due date and title asc) are same: Buy milk, Pack, Print. Not discriminating.\n\nUse query \"r\" and sort By title Ascending vs due date: Matches many; title asc order: Buy charger, Charge phone (+Buy charger?), Pack for trip, Pay rent, Print tickets, Read docs. Due date asc: Buy milk? no r? Buy milk notes \"2 litres\" has r, so matches Buy milk (Thu); Pack for trip (Nov 24); Pay rent (Dec 4); then no due ones (Charge phone, Print tickets, Read docs). With grouping None, no-due placement uncertain.\n\nMaybe set sort By due date Descending: no-due placement might be first (as seen when grouping Descending). Let's empirically compare.\n\nLet's do it. Set Sorting By due date, direction Descending. Grouping None. Close. Note list order. Then search \"r\" and note order. If the search result order differs from list order, likely bug in ranking.\n\nCurrently in sort sheet with Smart sort. Let's set sorting to By due date and direction Descending. Tap Sorting row, select By due date, then toggle direction to Descending if needed.\n\nTap row 4."}
