{"type": "thinking", "text": "Now search \"r\" results top: Read docs, Buy charger, QA tomorrow, QA year, RR child, Pay rent (Dec 4), Pack for trip (Nov 24)... So no-due tasks first (Read docs, Buy charger, QA tomorrow, QA year, RR child) then due date desc (Pay rent Dec 4, Pack Nov 24, Buy milk Thu, ...). This matches the list order (which also had no-due tasks in some order then due tasks). In list, the no-due order was Read docs, Buy charger, QA month, QA next, QA plain, ... QA tomorrow, QA top, QA year, QQ child, RR child... For search \"r\", the no-due order is Read docs, Buy charger, QA tomorrow, QA year, RR child. Hmm, does that match the list's relative order? In list, Read docs (1), Buy charger (2), ..., QA tomorrow (near 10), QA year (12), QQ child (13), RR child (14). Search shows Read docs, Buy charger, QA tomorrow, QA year, RR child — same relative order (with non-matching tasks removed). Good. Then due date desc. So search ranking by sort mode is correct.\n\nSo search ranking appears implemented as intended. Maybe there is no bug there. But we should test other search aspects: search ranking when sort is By title Descending we already did. Good.\n\nMaybe the seeded bug is that search ranking is by sort mode *active in the task list*, but if the search is initiated from a different list (e.g., \"Search\" from drawer) it uses default sort? Not likely.\n\nNow, the subtask badge bug we found might actually be the seeded bug for \"subtask badges computed from full list of nested tasks\". The developer confirmed. Fine.\n\nLet's do some more subtask tests for badge correctness:\n- Collapse Pack for trip and check badge count while subtasks collapsed. With the newly attached? Currently Charge phone has no children. Pack badge 2. Good.\n- Complete a subtask and check badge count: Does it count completed? In Tasks.org, badge likely counts *incomplete* subtasks only? Let's test: Complete \"Print tickets\" under Pack. Collapse Pack and see badge. If it goes from 2 to 1, badge counts incomplete. If stays 2, counts all. Which is correct? The changelog says \"badge number is now computed from the full list of nested tasks\" — could mean includes completed. Hmm, ambiguous. But typical Tasks.org behavior: badge shows number of *hidden* subtasks, including completed? Let me recall Tasks.org source: The badge is `subtaskCount` maybe number of incomplete subtasks. In many apps, the badge shows incomplete subtasks. If completed, they may be hidden but still count? Not sure.\n\nLet's test and reason from the requirement: \"Subtask badges on task rows: the badge number is now computed from the full list of nested tasks.\" That phrasing suggests the badge counts all nested tasks (including deeper levels), as opposed to only direct children. It doesn't say incomplete. The \"full list of nested tasks\" likely means all descendants, regardless of completion. The original data: Pack for trip has 2 subtasks, badge 2. Our nested added Buy charger made badge 3. That matches \"full list.\"\n\nWhat about hidden completed subtasks? If we complete Print tickets and show completed subtasks is ON, it's still visible; badge may still count 2. Not a clear bug. Skip.\n\n- Check badge shows on collapsed parent when it has nested tasks with different lists? Not relevant.\n\n- Check badge and \"Show completed subtasks\" off: if we turn off \"Show completed subtasks\" and complete Charge phone, Pack's badge should...? Hmm.\n\nMaybe the seeded badge bug: When \"Show completed subtasks\" is off and a completed subtask exists, the badge still counts it (shouldn't?). Ambiguous.\n\nLet's not over-test ambiguous stuff. We already have one confirmed bug. Maybe there are more seeded bugs in sort/grouping/search. Let's focus on finding concrete inconsistencies.\n\nIdea: Sort sheet direction fallback could cause a bug where the *list* uses a different direction than displayed after changing sort mode via the sheet while a *search* is active. Or when searching, the results use a stale direction. Hmm.\n\nAnother idea: \"Task search results: matches are now ranked by the sort mode active in the task list rather than by relevance.\" This may introduce a bug: search results are ranked by title/due date but *not grouped*, or the search results show subtasks as separate top-level matches. But the requirement says ranked by sort mode, so not a bug.\n\nWait, maybe the seeded search bug: Search results include tasks that don't match, or omit matches due to ranking. Let's test searching \"2 litres\" notes. Search \"litres\" → Buy milk. Search \"milk\" → Buy milk. Search \"trip\" → Pack. Search \"rent\" → Pay rent. Search \"read\" → Read docs. Search \"phone\" → Charge phone. Search \"print\" → Print tickets. Search \"guide\" → Read docs. Let's test maybe one fails due to ranking? Unlikely.\n\nAnother potential bug: Search with \"No due date\" grouping and sort by due date desc might exclude tasks without due date? No.\n\nMaybe the search results are ranked by sort mode but when sort is \"My order\", not. Hmm.\n\nLet's test search \"e\" again with current sort (By due date Descending, grouping None) and see if results order matches list. We just did \"r\". Fine.\n\nWhat about search result \"There are no tasks here.\" when no match, we verified.\n\nMaybe test search while in \"By priority\" sort with priorities set. But time.\n\nLet's investigate the \"direction preference loaded with a new fallback value\" more. Could the bug be visible in the grouping direction when grouping changes from None to By due date? When grouping None, the direction preference may be loaded with fallback and then when re-enabling grouping, the direction shows the fallback (Ascending) rather than the user's previous choice (Descending). We noticed earlier: grouping was Descending before setting None; after setting None and back to By due date, grouping showed Ascending, not the previous Descending. Is that a bug? Maybe the preference isn't persisted across \"None\" toggles. Hmm, but each grouping mode may have separate direction preferences: None has no direction, By due date has its own saved direction. When we switched from By due date Descending to None, then back, it showed Ascending (default) — so the By due date direction preference was lost. That could be the \"direction preference loaded with new fallback value\" bug: the grouping direction preference isn't restored; it falls back to Ascending even though the user had chosen Descending.\n\nSimilarly, sorting direction: We had Sorting By due date Descending at some point, then switched to By title; then when switching back to By due date, it showed Ascending (default), not the previous Descending. Is per-mode direction expected to be remembered? In Tasks.org, I believe the direction is a single global preference per sort mode group? Hmm. Let's think: The sort sheet has one \"Sorting\" row with a direction toggle. It might store direction per sort method (e.g., due date ascending, title descending). When you switch from title back to due date, the app might restore due date's previous direction. If it instead resets to fallback Ascending, that's the \"new fallback\" bug. But is losing the direction preference a \"bug\"? The changelog explicitly says \"the direction preference is now loaded with a new fallback value\", implying direction prefs are loaded and there's a fallback for missing values. If the fallback overrides saved values, that's a bug.\n\nWe need to test: Set Sorting By due date Descending, then switch to By title, then switch back to By due date. Does it restore Descending or show Ascending? Earlier (around the beginning): We had Sorting By due date Descending (initial). Then set By title → showed Ascending (expected default because title never had a direction? or because switching resets). Then toggled title to Descending. Then set By due date → showed Ascending (not the previous Descending). Then toggled to... hmm.\n\nIf the intended behavior is to remember direction per sort mode, then \"By due date\" should have remembered Descending. The fact it showed Ascending suggests it doesn't remember. But is that expected? Maybe the app stores a single direction preference globally, and changing sort mode resets to its default. In Tasks.org, I believe the direction ", "truncated": true}
