{"type": "thinking", "text": "Badge on Pack for trip = 4 (Charge phone, AA child, BB grandchild, Print tickets). Charge phone = 2 (AA child, BB grandchild). AA child = 1. So full nested count works, even two levels deep. Good.\n\nNow this is exactly the changelog feature working. The only bug is the detach-on-new-task-save (which affects badges). The developer confirmed.\n\nBut wait — the nested chain AA child/BB grandchild still exists. If we create a new task, BB grandchild might detach? The detach seemed to affect the most recently added subtask (the deepest? or the last created). In previous cases the last created subtask detached (Buy charger, ZZ sub, QQ child, RR child, SS child, TT child). Here the last created is BB grandchild. If we create a new task, maybe BB grandchild detaches, and Pack badge drops from 4 to 3. That would further confirm. But we already have confirmation. Don't need.\n\nWe need to clean up all temp tasks before finishing. That's a lot. But maybe we can leave? The instructions say \"Do not change data outside what testing requires.\" Cleanup is good practice. But deleting 20 tasks will take many steps. We have plenty of steps? The session has been long (maybe ~100 tool calls). There's no explicit limit, but token budget matters. Let's estimate: each task delete: tap row, tap Delete task, confirm dialog maybe. Could be 3 calls each → 60 calls. Too many. Maybe we can avoid cleanup? The QA instruction says don't change data outside what testing requires — our changes were for testing. Leaving temp tasks could be considered polluting, but the session ends after sign-off and no new build; maybe not critical. However, professional QA should restore the list. Hmm.\n\nWe could delete tasks via multi-select? Is there a bulk delete in Tasks.org? Long-press a task might allow multi-select. There's an ImageView \"More options\" at top and bottom. The bottom \"More options\" (3-dot) may have \"Select\" or \"Delete completed\". Not bulk delete of arbitrary tasks. The top-right \"More options\" (gear?) is settings. Hmm.\n\nMaybe there's a \"multi-select\" mode via long-press. Let's test long-press a task row on a temp task. If multi-select appears, we can select all temp tasks and delete. That could be efficient. But selecting 20 tasks via long-press and taps is still many steps but fewer than per-task dialogs maybe.\n\nAlternatively, we can use the app's \"More options\" menu → maybe \"Select\" or \"Delete\". Let's check the bottom More options (element 34). But careful: bottom 3-dot may be \"More options\" for sort or list. Let's inspect menu. Actually earlier the bottom toolbar: hamburger, search, sort, 3-dot, FAB. The 3-dot might open a menu with \"Select\" / \"New list\" etc.\n\nBefore cleanup, let's finish remaining tests:\n1. Search with special URL query (quick).\n2. Subtask badge when collapsed: Pack badge 4 collapsed; expand shows 4. Fine.\n3. Toggle \"Show completed subtasks\" off/on while a completed subtask exists: maybe badge behavior. Could reveal bug: badge should count only visible? Let's test quickly: Complete \"BB grandchild\" (deepest), Pack badge? Then toggle Show completed subtasks off. Hmm. Might be ambiguous.\n\nActually, the badge rule \"full list of nested tasks\" suggests badge counts all nested tasks. Let's test completed: complete BB grandchild, badge Pack should remain 4 if counting all, or 3 if incomplete-only. The changelog says full list of nested tasks, likely includes completed. But is \"full list\" meaning all levels or all including completed? Hmm. In Tasks.org source, the badge might be \"number of incomplete subtasks\" and the change is to count nested. The phrase \"full list of nested tasks\" more likely refers to the entire nested set (all descendants) vs direct children. It doesn't clarify completion. We might not want to report ambiguous.\n\n4. Sort direction: maybe test toggling direction while search active and verifying results update; quick.\n\n5. Search special char \"https://example.com/guide\" maybe. Let's do a quick search test for special chars. But search results currently? We are on list. Could skip.\n\nLet's consider other obvious defects in search:\n- When search is active, the sort sheet and grouping still work? We used sort? Not while search active. Search button remains; we can open sort while search active; maybe grouping/sorting apply? Not needed.\n- Search field with leading/trailing spaces: query \" milk \" might fail. Test? Could be considered.\n- Search from a filtered list: not relevant.\n\nThe QA sweep might have a seeded bug in search ranking: \"matches are now ranked by the sort mode active in the task list rather than by relevance.\" Could the bug be that the search results are sorted by the *opposite* direction? Let's verify more rigorously with a sort where direction is Ascending and query matches multiple due-date tasks. We did title Ascending? No. We did title Descending and due date Descending. Let's test title Ascending with \"e\" and verify order ascending. If sort direction ignored (always desc), that's a bug. Earlier we set By title Descending, results descending. Let's set By title Ascending, grouping None, search \"e\": expected Buy milk, Charge phone, Pay rent, Print tickets, Read docs (alphabetical asc). Actually with nested: Buy milk (B), Charge phone (C) with Buy charger child (B), Pay rent (P a), Print tickets (P r), Read docs (R). We can check. If results come descending, bug. Let's test. This is a good consistency test.\n\nSteps: open sort, set Sorting By title (direction defaults Ascending), keep grouping None. Close. Open search, type \"e\". Observe order. Let's do. Current list grouping By due date Ascending; we need grouping None too for clarity. Set both.\n\nOpen sort (33)."}
